@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Privacy notice";

    var signedIn = User.Identity?.IsAuthenticated == true;

    Layout = signedIn
        ? "~/Views/Shared/_Layout.cshtml"
        : "~/Views/Shared/_PublicLayout.cshtml";
}

<section class="band">

    <div class="band-inner prose">

        @* Home/Index serves the dashboard to anybody signed in and
           the front page to everybody else, so one link is right for
           both readers -- only the wording changes. *@
        <a class="btn btn-outline-secondary mb-4"
           asp-controller="Home"
           asp-action="Index">
            <i class="fas fa-arrow-left" aria-hidden="true"></i>
            @(signedIn ? "Back to dashboard" : "Back to home")
        </a>

        <span class="eyebrow">Privacy</span>

        <h1 class="prose-title">How this portal uses your information</h1>

        <p class="prose-lede">
            My Intellego is the staff portal of Intellego Investment
            Consultants (Pvt) Ltd. It holds employment records for
            people who work here, so that leave, training, documents
            and related requests can be handled in one place.
        </p>

        <h2>What the portal holds about you</h2>

        <p>
            Your record is created by HR when you join, and is added
            to as you use the portal.
        </p>

        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Who you are.</strong> Name, employee number,
                work email, phone number, national identity number,
                date of birth, gender, marital status and home
                address.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Your job.</strong> Job title, department,
                employment type, the date you joined, and where
                relevant a contract end date or leaving date and
                reason.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Your next of kin.</strong> The name,
                relationship, phone number and address of the person
                to contact in an emergency, together with the date you
                last confirmed those details.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>What you have asked for.</strong> Leave
                requests and balances, loan applications and any
                receipts or quotations attached to them, course
                applications, and the decisions taken on each.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Your training and documents.</strong> Training
                assigned to you and any feedback you give,
                certifications recorded against your name, documents
                HR holds for you, and policies you have acknowledged.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Company property.</strong> Assets issued to
                you.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Your sign-in.</strong> Your account, the roles
                it carries, and a protected form of your password. The
                portal never stores your password itself.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Why it is held</h2>

        <p>
            To administer your employment: to pay and account for
            leave, to route approvals to the right people, to meet the
            company's record-keeping obligations, and to reach
            somebody on your behalf in an emergency.
        </p>

        <h2>Who can see it</h2>

        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>You</strong> can see your own record and
                everything you have submitted.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>HR</strong> can see staff records, requests
                and documents, in order to administer them.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Approvers</strong> see the requests put in
                front of them for a decision, and the applicant's
                name and department alongside.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Administrators</strong> can see records and
                settings in order to keep the portal running.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Colleagues</strong> see only what the staff
                directory shows: name, job title, department and work
                contact details.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <p>
            Nothing in the portal is sold, and nothing is shared
            outside the company except where the company is required
            to do so by law or by a regulator.
        </p>

        <h2>What is recorded about your use</h2>

        <p>
            Actions that change a record are written to an audit log
            with your name and the time, so that decisions can be
            traced afterwards. Approval steps are also kept on the
            request itself, showing who decided what and when, and any
            comment they left.
        </p>

        <h2>How long it is kept</h2>

        <p>
            Records are kept for as long as you work here and
            afterwards for as long as the company is required to keep
            employment records. Documents with an expiry date are kept
            until they are superseded or no longer needed.
        </p>

        <h2>Keeping it accurate</h2>

        <p>
            You can correct most of your own details on My Profile.
            Anything you cannot change yourself, including your name,
            employee number and employment dates, is maintained by HR
            on request.
        </p>

        <h2>Who to ask</h2>

        <p>
            Questions about this notice, about what is held about you,
            or a request to see or correct it should go to HR. If you
            are not satisfied with the answer, raise it with the
            Managing Director.
        </p>

        <p class="prose-note">
            This notice describes how the portal works today. It is
            not legal advice and has not been reviewed by a legal
            adviser. The company should have whoever is responsible
            for data protection check it against the Data Protection
            Act [Chapter 11:12] and its own policies before relying
            on it.
        </p>

    </div>

</section>
